  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 1225 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe bulk of the seven day forecast remains the epitome of the old \nforecaster's adage - Big Bubble, No Trouble.\n\nDry weather is forecast throughout the next seven days due to the \nexpansive influence of strong surface high pressure. Broadly \ncyclonic flow will be in place over the region, though any forcing \nfrom shortwave troughs pivoting through this flow will be displaced \nwell to the north, leaving the primary impact to the area to be mild \ntemperatures and low humidity throughout the period.\n\nTemperatures will be mild and pleasant, with substantially less \nhumid conditions than experienced in recent weeks. This leaves the \nprimary weather hazard of concern as fire conditions, with afternoon \nrelative humidity values likely to drop into the 30 percent range on \nmany if not most days, and possibly even lower as blended guidance \noften struggles to fully capture the degree of mixing out of \ndewpoints in the afternoons with plentiful sunshine, even in \nsettings with relatively light surface flow.\n\nAdditionally, there is a low chance for patchy frost mainly \nnortheast Monday night into Tuesday morning as lows will have the \npotential to drop into the upper 30s. Current numbers appear too \nwarm to support anything widespread or significant, but if we can \ndrop a couple/few more degrees in the outlying areas this may be on \nthe table.\n\nIn any case, several days of pleasant Autumn weather are expected \nover the next week, and all are encouraged to enjoy the prolonged \nperiod of mild fall days and cool nights.",
